  "body": "### What problem are you trying to solve?\n\nDeveloper experience issue:\r\nFor code generating tabular data, with cells spanning all/remaining columns, you can easily end up with wrong colspan if the code surrounding the table generation changes. There should be easier away to specify that you want a cell to span all /remaining cells to simplify this use case, hence the intent is always kept across changes to the rest of the table.\n\n### What solutions exist today?\n\nToday rowspan=\"0\" is supported to denote a way to span all remaining rows.\r\n\r\nFor colspan a common hack is to set colspan=\"100%\", which will be understood as 100, but on larger tables a overflow colspan value will trigger performance issues in Firefox's rendering engine.\n\n### How would you solve it?\n\nFormalize colspan=\"0\" in the spec to harmonize meaning with usage on rowspan.\r\n\r\nMeaning: Same as rowspan=0 but for columns: Span all / remaining columns\n\n### Anything else?\n\nDefine what should happen if there is both rowspan=\"0\" and colspan=\"0\" used on a table.\r\n\r\nCould be: First wins (meaning rowspan=0 wins)",
